Linux basic commmand:

Username  @  Hostname  :~  #
  1             2      3   4

  1: name of logged in User
  2: Machine name
  3: folder name
  4: user type
  # : root user
  $ : normal user


  windows               Linux
  files                 files
  Folders               Directory
  Admin                 root user/super user
  software              package

  1. sudo su - : (Super User DO) (Switch User) /  sudo -i
  ---> access root account
  syntax: sudo su -

  2. man man : manual
  --> display manual page
  syntax: man man

  3. pwd: Present Working Directory
  --> disply current working directory
  syntax: pwd
  
  4. ls : List
  --> used to display current dircetory/folder contents
  i] syntax: ls
  ii] syntax: ls <dir_name>
  --> to checkout specific dir contents
  eg:  ls /etc

  5. clear:
  --> to clear screen
  syntax: clear
  OR use ctrl+l

  6. mkdir: Make Directory
  --> used to create directory
  i] syntax: mkdir <folder_name>
  eg:  mkdir demo

  ii] syntax: mkdir <folder1> <folder2> <folder3>
  --> used to create multiple folder
  eg: mkdir test folder1 folder2

  iii] syntax: mkdir -p <parent/child>
  --> used to create nested directories
  eg: mkdir -p env/test

  7. cd : Change Directory
  --> used to move between directories
  syntax: cd <folder_name>
  eg: cd env

  ii] syntax: cd ..
  --> takes you to previous directory

  8.  rmdir:
  --> to delete empty directory
  syntax: rmdir <dir_name>
  eg: rmdir demo
  syntax: rmdir <dir1> <dir2>
  eg: rmdir folder1 folder2

  9. rm -rf:
  --> used to delete non empty directory
  syntax: rm -rf <dir_name>
  here,
  rm stands for remove
  -r stands for recursive
  -f stands for forecfully

-r tells rm to recursively delete the directory and all its contents
"recursive means that the command will operate on a directory and all of its sub directories and files"
eg: rm -rf 

10. touch:
--> used to create empty file.
syntax: touch <filename>
eg: touch test.txt

11. cat > <file_name>:
--> create file and add contents into file.
syntax: cat > <file_name>
eg: cat >test
"use ctrl+d to save and exit"

12. cat :
--> to display file contents
syntax: cat <file_name>
eg: cat file100

13. cp : Copy
--> used to copy file
syntax: cp <source_file> <destination>
eg: cp demo.txt folder1

14. cp -r :
--> used to copy dircetory
syntax: cp -r <source_folder> <destination_folder>
eg: cp -r folder1 folder2

15. mv : move
--> used to move files/dirctory
syntax: mv <source> <destination_folder>
eg: mv demo.txt folder2

16. wc: Word Count
--> used to display no of lines, word count and character count
syntax: wc <file_name>
eg: wc demo.txt

17. head: 
--> to display first few(10 lines) lines of files.
syntax: head <file_name>
eg: head demo.txt
syntax: head -n <file_name>
--> to display n lines of file
eg: head -5 demo.txt

18. tail:
--> to display last few lines(10 lines) of file.
syntax: tail <file_name>
eg: tail demo.txt

19. sort:
-->used to sort file
syntax: sort <file_name>
eg: sort demo.txt

20. uniq:
--> used to delete all adjacent duplicate lines.
syntax: uniq <file_name>
eg: uniq demo.txt

sort file100 | uniq

| = pipe
--> it is used to connect the output of one command to the input of another command
eg: command1 | command2
--> instaed of displaying the output of command1 on the screen, linux passes it directly to
the command2










